"36. Are there circumstances under which Reg E-Delivery should permit a covered entity to use an electronic address for a covered recipient that the covered entity receives from an affiliate or from a third party, rather than directly from the covered recipient? If so, what are those circumstances and how would permitting such use be consistent with the goal of ensuring that covered recipients who provide an electronic address are aware of how the electronic address will be used and what types of covered information will be electronically delivered to that electronic address? For example, are there circumstances under which an underwriter of a securities offering should be permitted to use an electronic address provided to the issuer in the offering, or vice versa? Or, if a covered recipient is onboarded though one covered entity but will receive products or services from another, affiliated covered entity that is part of the same enterprise, should the latter covered entity be permitted to use the electronic address that the covered recipient provided to the former covered entity? Or if a covered recipient is onboarded through one Conformed to Federal Register version covered entity that contracts with another, unaffiliated covered entity to provide services for the covered recipient (e.g., an introducing broker-dealer contracting with a clearing broker-dealer, or a separately managed account program sponsor contracting with an investment adviser managing accounts in the program), should the latter covered entity be permitted to use the electronic address that the covered recipient provided to the former covered entity?"

It should be permissible for a service provider to transmit an electronic address for contact verification, but covered entities can not entirely rely on that data share and consider it a validated contact. Before replacing paper or another established channel, covered entities must establish that the email address is current and controlled by the recipient. An opt out of electronic delivery should be plainly available to all relevant parties and covered entities must and retain evidence of both their offering the choice to opt out to recipients along with the recipient’s choice. Scenarios including failed electronic delivery a disputed address should result in manual followup. It is also important that opting out of electronic delivery does not immediately result in accuring fees or experiencing a loss of services. It is a fact that paper records and hard copies are not as scalable as electronic, and that over time an adjustment to electronic transimission as the norm is warranted. The change should be careful and considerate to the many investors who continue to operate in the paper and legacy space. Maintaining freely available paper communications is crucial while investors contemplate adjusting. The proposed rule requiring notice 180 days before transition plus a follow-up notice 30 days before transition is adequate, and while applies to the defined existing paper-recipient transition[^1], it should also apply to every newly onboarded investor to ensure all future directly registered investors have the same choice when considering communication preference.

There are circumstances where covered entitys should be able to use confirmed emails through other means beyond the investor themselves - but crucially, it must be verifiable through association with other known identifying information and in line with legal avenues for storing and reviewing that identifying information. There is no use in expanding information access in name of cost savings and efficiency if inaccuracy is introduced, to say nothing of the negative impact that would have on investors. This adjustment would be best considered as a proactive measure to ensure decentralization and scalability for operators within the transfer agent space, as while the robust legacy methods of contact and confirmation have worked well, there has been massive technological development within the space and commisurate with pending transfer agent regulation adjustments on the horizon per S7-2026-30 there should be changes made to prioritize easier compliance for TAs and to increase communication access for investors. 

While on the whole we agree with the concept of utilizing specifically email, there are some concerns if a third party would be allowed to provide a otherwise nonverified email address or similar electronic contact to a transfer agent to satisfy recordkeeping regulations and with expectation that the transfer agent would then use that provided contact in order to manage communications with the directly registered owner. However, some guardrails are absolutely necessary. While it would be convenient to jump straight to accepting these types of contacts through a third party for ease of transition and transfer, any removal of specific physical verification of the owner involved could introduce more distance between the managing transfer agent and the owner or limit the ability of the owner to make informed and current decisions on their directly held investments. 

Concerning the shape of these guardrails: There must be an opt-out notice as part of the transition to this policy, and the transition to this policy should be on a longer time scale for transfer agents. Individual investors should be prompted with the opportunity to preserve traditional paper contact and, if they see the value in moving to e-delivery, information should be traditionally validated through scalable confirmation outreach. One example could be having entities send out a paper mail which would verify information about the recipient before providing details about the transition to the new information policies, giving the recipient the opportunity to provide such information directly and select preferences, and a timeline for the transition. Popups can be implemented on apps and on web clients. Considering the sizable potential savings available by transitioning to predominately e-delivery, it is worth the initial investment for covered entities to go through this process, and for the proposal to details 180 days with a followup 30 days before the transition. A generous window of time can be provided so that there is time for design and implementation of these tools. As long as investors have an opportunity to review the information and to opt out, and that covered entities reach out to those investors for that clarity, then the industry can move forward into a more technologically friendly status quo that better reflects the modern day. What must be avoided is the possibility that a large amount of investors are lost in this transition - covered entities must be diligent in tracking their outreach, and in maintaining a record of that outreach. That record should be available to regulators and to account holders. Processes which are currently utilized to locate dormant account holders leading into escheatment can be used for this purpose, which should limit the costs to the covered entities.

In securities-offering scenarios where participating entities in information exchanges are mostly dealing with beneficial owners and account holders rather than direct investors the process can be simpler. Have entities adjust their terms of service policies and contracts to reflect that information may be shared with other relevant entities and require that investors are made directly aware of this change. In addition to those top level changes, there should be specific notice to effected individuals detailing which information is on file, that it may be shared, and a cursory provided notice whenever it is shared with other entities. These are all useful preventative measurese to avoid confusion. Persons should still be offered an opt out, and they must be clearly informed what kind of information qualifies to be shared and with which entities that information may be shared with. 

[^1]: https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/proposed/2026/33-11430.pdf pp. 96–97 and 372–375.
